# debuger

A modern, good-looking CLI to debug C/C++ programs with a friendly UX, clear output, and portable backends. The goal is to give you the speed and power of native debuggers (LLDB, GDB, WinDbg/CDB) with a simple, cohesive interface that feels great in a terminal.

This repository currently contains the initial documentation for architecture, CLI design, and a roadmap. Implementation will begin after validating this plan.

- Primary target: C++ on Windows, Linux, and macOS
- Adapters: LLDB (primary), GDB (fallback), WinDbg/CDB (Windows-specific, later milestone)
- Nice-by-default output with colors, readable backtraces, pretty-printed variables, and helpful hints

See:
- docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
- docs/CLI.md
- docs/ROADMAP.md
- docs/INTEGRATION_WINDOWS.md

## Quick Start (planned)

- `debuger init` — scaffold a `debuger.yaml` in your project
- `debuger run` — launch under the debugger with symbols
- `debuger attach --pid <PID>` — attach to a running process
- `debuger bt` — print a clean backtrace
- `debuger step` / `next` / `cont` — step control

For your Visual Studio project at `C:\\Users\\Antoine\\source\\repos\\ConsoleApplication1`, point `debuger.yaml` at the built executable (e.g. `x64\\\\Debug\\\\ConsoleApplication1.exe`), or run with `debuger open <path-to-exe>`.

## Status

- This is the planning and documentation phase.
- Early implementation will target LLDB (if present) and fall back to GDB. Native Windows adapter for PDB via CDB/WinDbg comes later.

